### Phase 2.2 — Ring-3 userspace LIVE (2026-09-24)
Verified via embedded `hello` ELF64 (crt0.asm + libc.c `_syscall` SysV ABI):
- `create_user_hello` -> scheduler -> trampoline iretq -> Ring-3 CR0 WP alive
- `Hello from User Space!` (write), `My PID is: 1` (getpid), `Sleeping for 1 second...`
  (sleep: pass through syscall_dispatch dispatch) `Done! Exiting.` -> socket? No -> sys_exit ->
  `[PROC] 'hello' (PID 1) exited with code 0` -> zombie -> context switch -> shell `myos>`
- Build gate clean (-Wall -Wextra -Werror), boot gate ok (no #GP/#PF/#UD).

### Phase 2.3 — Bug found & fixed: swapgs/GS-BASE vs active %gs (2026-09-24)
Symptom: #GP(13) inside syscall_entry64 at `mov %r15,(%rcx)` with rcx bogus; diagnostics
showed `GS_BASE=0 KERNEL_GS=0` and `gs:0=0xf000ff53f000ff53`.
- probe: after `wrmsr(IA32_GS_BASE, &syscall_cpu)` a C `movq %%gs:0` read BACK **0** (expected 0x28a450).
  => On this QEMU/kernel the architectural MSR write does NOT update the active %gs segment
  base, so any `%gs:0`-relative per-CPU scheme is dead on arrival here.
- Fix: syscall entry no longer uses GS. `syscall_cpu[8]` is absolute-address memory
  (extern symbol in context_switch.asm): [0]=kernel stack top, [1..4]=entry scratch.
  Context_switch.asm: swapgs removed from syscall_entry64, usermode_enter_trampoline,
  enter_usermode, and isr64.asm (reverted to original entry/exit, no CS&3 check needed).
  MSR_GS_BASE / MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE still programmed (future-proof), not relied on.
- isr.c debug GS/PTE introspection trimmed to keep only usable fault diagnostics
  (RIP/RSP/CS/err print, CR2 + PML4/PDPT/PD/PT walk for #14).

